With the project, personnel, taxa, sites, and events in place, entering a
specimen record is mostly a matter of selecting from lists and typing
measurements. Locality, coordinates, collection method, and eligible collectors
all flow automatically from the event you choose.

In this tutorial, you will enter the seven specimens selected in
[Basic tutorial 1](/en/tutorials/basic/paper-catalog/). Each illustrates a
different paper-catalog failure mode and shows how to choose and document the
best-supported value.

## From paper form to NAHPU

| Paper box | NAHPU field | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Collector No.` | Field ID | Heru's initials and number, shown as `HH436` |
| `Museum` | `Tissue ID` (on the `Liver` part) | Young Heru wrote tissue numbers (`Z26230`, …) in the `Museum` box; leave `Museum ID` (under `Show more`) empty |
| `Species` | `Taxon` | Selected from the taxon registry |
| `Locality`, `Trap or net line` | `Site ID`, then `Event ID` | In the capture record |
| `Capture date`, `Time` | `Capture date`, `Capture time` | Also enter the date as `Collection date` |
| `Collection method` | `Method` | Restricted to the event's methods |
| `Collector` | `Collector` | Restricted to the event's personnel |
| `TTL`, `TL`, `HF`, `Ear`, `WT (FA)` | Total, tail, hind foot, and ear length; weight | Head-and-body length is calculated automatically |
| `male` / `female` and reproductive boxes | `Sex` and reproductive data | Single sex; reproductive fields adapt to it |
| `Age` | `Life stage` | |
| `Preparation Code`, `Tissues in` | `Specimen Parts` | One part record per piece of material |
| `Habitat` | Site habitat | Already entered in Tutorial 3 |
| `Remarks` | `Remarks` | |

The paper forms include no legend for the preparation codes. These tutorials
interpret them as:

- `SA`: study skin with cleaned, dry skull and skeleton;
- `AL`: whole animal fixed in formalin (fluid specimen).

The `Tissues in` box indicates a tissue sample preserved in ethanol, entered
here as `Liver`.

Before you begin, confirm that `Skin`, `Skull`, `Skeleton`, `Whole body`, and
`Liver` exist under `Settings` > `Specimens` > `Specimen types`, and that
`None`, `ETOH`, and `Formalin` exist under `Treatments`.

## 1. Enter HH 436, the complete record

HH 436 is a squirrel caught on 12 September, the day the lines were set. The
journal for that day reads, "I caught a squirrel right after setting the
traps." Both sources agree.

{% steps %}

1. Open `Specimens` and select `+`. Confirm that the Field ID reads `HH436`; if
   not, use `Edit field ID` to fix it.

    NAHPU fills each Field ID from Heru's cataloger number and increments it by
    one for every new specimen. In real fieldwork, this keeps numbers in
    sequence automatically. Because these tutorials skip numbers—after HH 437,
    NAHPU proposes `HH438`, whereas the next tutorial specimen is HH 442—use
    `Edit field ID` whenever the proposed number differs from the paper.

2. In `Collection & Identification`:

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | `Cataloger` | `Heru Handika` (also fills `Preparator` and `Determiner`) |
    | `Museum ID` | Leave empty |
    | `Collection date`, `Collection time` | 12 September 2014, `16:00` |
    | `Taxon` | *Lariscus insignis* |
    | `ID Confidence` | `High` |

    Set `Prep. date` to 12 September 2014. Heru prepared specimens the same day
    they were caught, but the paper does not record preparation times, so leave
    `Prep. time` empty.

    {% aside type="note" title="Why the Z numbers are not Museum IDs" %}
    Young Heru wrote `Z26230` (and the rest of the `Z` series) in the paper
    form's `Museum` box—another mistake in the 2014 catalog. Those `Z` numbers
    are **tissue numbers**, not museum catalog numbers. Museums usually do not
    assign a museum catalog number until a specimen arrives at the collection
    and is formally accessioned. Leave `Museum ID` empty here and enter each
    `Z` number as `Tissue ID` on the `Liver` part below.
    {% /aside %}

3. In the capture record, select `Site ID` `TJHH-L1`, then its event, and enter:

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | `Capture date`, `Capture time` | 12 September 2014, `16:00` |
    | `Method` | `Snap trap` |
    | `Coordinate ID` | `TJHH-PM` |
    | `Collector` | `Heru Handika` |

4. Enter the measurements: total length `238`, tail `84`, hind foot `46`, ear
   `18`, weight `120` g. Keep `Accuracy` as `Accurate`.
5. Set `Sex` to `Male`, testis `Position` to `Abdominal`, and `Life stage` to
   `Subadult`.
6. Under `Specimen Parts`, add four parts: `Skin`, `Skull`, and `Skeleton` (each
   with treatment `None`), plus `Liver` with treatment `ETOH` and `Tissue ID`
   `Z26230`. The opaque code `SA` becomes three explicit parts that can be
   stored, loaned, and tracked independently.

{% /steps %}

Reopen the record: without typing a locality, it displays Indonesia, Jambi,
Kerinci, Pondok Merah, the coordinate, and the habitat—all inherited from the
site. (The screenshot below shows `Z26230` in `Museum ID` from an earlier
draft; in your record, `Museum ID` stays empty and `Z26230` lives on the
`Liver` part's `Tissue ID`.)

![NAHPU Specimen Records page on an iPad for HH436, Lariscus insignis, with collection on 12 September 2014 at 4:00 PM, preparation date 12 September 2014 with no preparation time, site TJHH-L1, method Snap trap, and coordinate TJHH-PM](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/specimen-hh436.webp)

Further down the record, NAHPU calculates head-and-body length (154 mm) and the
tail ratio from the measurements you entered:

![NAHPU Specimen Attributes and Specimen Parts for HH436 on an iPad: total length 238, tail 84, hind foot 46, ear 18, weight 120 g, male, subadult; parts Skin, Skull, and Skeleton with no treatment, and Liver in ETOH](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/specimen-hh436-attributes.webp)

## 2. Enter the remaining six records

Follow the same steps with the values below, leaving `Museum ID` empty on every
record and placing each `Z` tissue number in the `Liver` part's `Tissue ID`.
The notes after the table explain each decision.

| Field ID | `Tissue ID` | `Taxon` | Site | Capture date and time | `Method` | `Collector` | TTL–TL–HF–Ear, weight | `Sex` | `Life stage` | Parts |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `HH437` | `Z26231` | *Mus crociduroides* | `TJHH-L1` | 13 Sep 2014, `07:00` | `Snap trap` | Heru Handika | 191–103–22–16, 16 g | `Female` | `Subadult` | Skin; skull; skeleton; liver, ETOH |
| `HH442` | See note | *Maxomys hylomyoides* | `TJHH-L1` | 13 Sep 2014, empty | `Snap trap` | Heru Handika | 238–119–30–18, 46 g | `Female` | Empty | Whole body, Formalin |
| `HH453` | `Z26247` | *Crocidura beccarii* | `TJHH-P1` | 13 Sep 2014, empty | `Pitfall` | Heru Handika | 150–75–17–8, 7 g | `Male` | `Adult` | Whole body, Formalin; liver, ETOH |
| `HH456` | `Z26250` | *Maxomys hylomyoides* | `TJBS-L2` | 13 Sep 2014, `07:00` | `Snap trap` | Heru Handika | 221–113–28–16, 48 g | `Male`, testes abdominal | `Subadult` | Whole body, Formalin; liver, ETOH |
| `HH523` | `Z26317` | *Maxomys* (genus) | `TJHH-L3` | 17 Sep 2014, `09:00` | `Snap trap` | Bima Sampel | 268–124–29–18, 66 g | `Male`, testes 17 × 4 mm | `Adult` | Skin; skull; skeleton; liver, ETOH |
| `HH529` | Empty | *Crocidura neglecta* | `TJHH-P4` | 17 Sep 2014, `07:00` | `Pitfall` | Bima Sampel | 112–45–11–7, 4.4 g | `Male` | `Adult` | Whole body, Formalin; liver, ETOH |

Copy each capture date to `Collection date` and `Prep. date`, and leave
`Prep. time` empty. For the two Pasir Putih specimens (`HH523` and `HH529`),
leave `Coordinate ID` empty because those sites have no coordinates. Override
the proposed Field ID for each record as noted in step 1.

{% aside type="note" title="Why collection and preparation times matter" %}
In genomic tissue collections, the interval between death and preservation
directly affects DNA and RNA quality. Recording both collection time and
preparation time helps curators and labs evaluate tissue suitability before
sequencing. Paper catalogs often omit this—Heru's 2014 forms record capture
times but not preparation times, because genomic work was far less accessible
then. NAHPU tracks both times on every specimen, and individual parts can also
record a sampling timestamp and post-mortem interval (`PMI`).
{% /aside %}

### HH 437: overwritten number and sex

On paper, `437` is written over `436`, and `male` is scribbled out with
`female` circled. Both edits look deliberate, so enter `HH437` and `Female`.
NAHPU makes these errors harder to introduce: Field IDs increment
automatically, and `Sex` holds one value—changing it prompts `Change sex?` and
clears mismatched reproductive fields instead of leaving both sets on the form.

Add to `Remarks`:

```text
Paper form: field number written over 436; sex corrected from male to female.
```

### HH 442: a date out of sequence

The form reads `17 Sept`, but three lines of evidence point to 13 September:

- the adjacent specimens (`HH 441` and `HH 443`) were both caught on 13
  September;
- its tissue number (`Z26236`, written in the `Museum` box) sits between theirs
  (`Z26235` and `Z26237`);
- the 17 September captures do not begin until `HH 512`.

Enter 13 September, and leave the blank time and life stage empty. Because
every specimen needs an event, link it to `TJHH-L1` (where `HH 438–441` were
caught) and document that inference. Because `Tissues in` is not circled, do
not add a liver part—note the uncircled `Z26236` tissue number in `Remarks`
instead.

Add to `Remarks`:

```text
Paper form gives capture date 17 Sep 2014; entered as 13 Sep 2014 from the record sequence and tissue numbers (Z26236 written in the Museum box, though Tissues in was not circled). Trap line not recorded; assigned to Line 1 as for HH 438–441.
```

### HH 453: a pitfall capture on "Line 1"

The form reads line `1` and method `Pitfall`. On paper, Line 1 held both
pitfalls and snap traps, so the line number alone is ambiguous. In NAHPU,
linking to `TJHH-P1` restricts `Method` to `Pitfall`. The neighboring record,
`HH 452`, shows why this matters: its method was originally written as
`Snap Trap`, then crossed out for `Pitfall`.

### HH 456: a changed identification

On paper, `Mus crociduroides` is crossed out and replaced with
`Maxomys hylomyoides`, with no determiner or date. Enter *Maxomys hylomyoides*
and set `ID Confidence` to `Medium` to reflect the field change. `Determiner`
already records who made the call.

Add to `Remarks`:

```text
First recorded in the field as Mus crociduroides.
```

When a specialist re-identifies the specimen later, they update `Taxon`,
`Determiner`, and `ID Confidence` in the same record.

### HH 523: a possible new species

Heru wrote `Maxomys cf. hylomyoides`—resembling *M. hylomyoides*, but
uncertain. Choose the genus *Maxomys* from the registry and set `ID Confidence`
to `High` because the genus is certain.

The form records `09:00` instead of the team's standard `07:00` overnight time;
enter `09:00` as written and note the difference. Because Heru stayed in camp
on 17 September while the boat crew checked Pasir Putih, choose `Bima Sampel`
as `Collector`.

Add to `Remarks`:

```text
cf. hylomyoides. Underparts darker than M. hylomyoides: more orange at the base and grey on the belly. Paper time 09:00 differs from the team's standard 07:00.
```

### HH 529: wrong collector and a misused box

The form lists `07.00`, the team's standard overnight capture time. Enter
`07:00`—the event notes from Tutorial 4 explain what it means—and choose
`Bima Sampel` as `Collector`, as for HH 523.

The `Museum` box reads `Blank Tube`—which makes sense once you know young Heru
was using that box for tissue tube numbers. Leave `Museum ID` and the liver
part's `Tissue ID` empty, and add `Tube label left blank in 2014.` to the liver
part's `Remarks`. Because NAHPU gives museum IDs, tissue numbers, and part
remarks their own dedicated fields, notes like this no longer hijack the wrong
box.

In the finished record, the site and event show Pasir Putih, and `Coordinate ID`
is empty because that site has no coordinate:

![NAHPU Specimen Records page on an iPad for HH529, Crocidura neglecta, captured on 17 September 2014 at 7:00 AM at TJHH-P4 by pitfall, with no coordinate](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/specimen-hh529.webp)

{% aside type="tip" title="Faster entry for similar specimens" %}
When many specimens share the same preparations, open a finished record and
select `Duplicate part` from the three-dot menu. NAHPU creates a new specimen
with the same part types and treatments while leaving tissue IDs, dates, and
remarks blank.
{% /aside %}

## 3. Check your records

{% steps %}

1. Turn on `Show outlier warnings` and review any flagged measurements.
2. Open each record and verify its Field ID against the paper catalog.
3. Confirm that the two Pasir Putih specimens (`HH523` and `HH529`) show Pasir
   Putih, not Pondok Merah.
4. Confirm that `HH453` and `HH529` show `Pitfall`, and the other five show
   `Snap trap`.

{% /steps %}

## What you gained over paper

- Specimens inherit locality from their site, so none can carry the wrong place.
- Method and collector come from the event, preventing pitfall captures from
  being logged as snap traps and ensuring the collector was actually present.
- Taxon names, sex, and life stage are chosen from controlled lists with one
  clean value.
- Every correction and inference is explained in `Remarks` rather than
  scribbled over.
- Parts are explicit records instead of undocumented preparation codes.

